# {{ ansible_managed }}
# =============================================================================
# Curated alarm — a container that exists but is not running (ADR-030, phase 2)
# =============================================================================
# The companion to homelab-container-unhealthy: `docker ps --filter
# health=unhealthy` only lists RUNNING containers, and the heal timer only
# resurrects ones that exited on their own. A container that fails to come back
# is invisible to both. That is how a stopped socket-proxy would go unnoticed
# while Traefik keeps answering 200 from its in-memory routes, blind to every
# container change (issue #34).
#
# `max -10m of running` is 0 only if the container was running at NO point in
# the window. The gate lives in the lookup for the same reason as the alarm next
# door: `delay` postpones a notification netdata never sends, and the adapter
# reads the status directly.
#
# --- The boot window: predicted here, then measured --------------------------
# This alarm is NOT equivalent to the check it replaced, and the difference was
# named here before it could surprise anyone. It has since been measured, and
# the prediction was wrong in the instructive direction.
#
# PREDICTED: homelab-health.sh gated its version on `mountpoint -q /mnt/data`,
# so it stayed silent between a reboot and the manual LUKS unlock and only then
# started its ten-minute clock. A netdata alarm cannot test a host mountpoint,
# and `max -10m` is only as long as the chart's own history — so a container
# still waiting for its startup wave looked likely to trip this early, leaving
# the monitor red for the length of the staged startup.
#
# MEASURED, over the reboots of 2026-08-26 10:04-10:28 and 2026-08-29 02:46: it
# never fired, and structurally cannot. Netdata is itself a container in the
# LAST startup wave, so by the time its docker collector has any chart at all,
# every container is already up. The boot-window noise this header predicted is
# unreachable.
#
# So the boot window is not noisy, it is BLIND — the opposite failure, and worth
# stating in those words because the two call for opposite answers. It is
# accepted. The case this alarm exists for (#34: socket-proxy stopped while
# Traefik kept answering 200 from its in-memory routes) is caught at full
# latency once netdata holds ten minutes of chart, and a container that fails to
# come back after a reboot stays down long enough to qualify. The one genuinely
# uncovered case — a container ABSENT from the engine — moved to the goss spec
# at daily rather than five-minute latency; that trade is recorded in the
# tombstone at the foot of homelab-health.sh.
#
# Phase 2 therefore DID delete, on 2026-08-26 (c2c2a36): both container blocks
# left homelab-health.sh once these observations were in. That is ADR-030's rule
# completing, not being waived. Nothing here is pending.
# =============================================================================

 template: homelab_container_down
       on: docker.container_state
    class: Errors
     type: Docker
component: Container
   lookup: max -10m unaligned of running
    units: boolean
    every: 1m
     warn: $this == 0
  summary: A container exists but has not been running for ten minutes
     info: Container present in the engine and not running for 10m — expected containers that are ABSENT entirely are covered by the goss spec, not here
       to: silent
